// The spatialindex package: where things are, asked three ways. An index
// holds items, each a box and a handle of the caller's choosing (any Copy
// type: a number, a pool.Ref, a part id), never the caller's objects; and
// answers which boxes a ray, a box, a ball or a camera's frustum meets,
// which are nearest a point, and which pairs overlap.
//
//   mod demo;
//   import geomath use *;
//   import spatialindex use *;
//
//   fn main() {
//     mut tree = Bvh[u64].create(0.1);              // fat boxes: 0.1 of margin
//     imm a = tree.insert(Aabb3.fromCenter(Vec3(0., 0., 0.), Vec3(1., 1., 1.)), 7u64);
//     mut hit = fn(h u64, p Proxy) bool { ...; true; };   // true goes on, false stops
//     tree.overlapSphere(Sphere(Vec3(2., 0., 0.), 1.5), &mut hit);
//     tree.move(a, newBox, displacement);           // tree changes only if it must
//     tree.remove(a);                               // a's Proxy is now dead
//   }
//
// THE THREE INDEXES (each its own file) have one interface, so that code can
// be written once for one and moved to another:
//
//   Bvh[T]          bvh.cone, bvhtree.cone. A dynamic AABB tree (the one in
//                   Box2D, Bullet and Jolt): items keep a fat box, so small
//                   moves change nothing; insertion by surface-area cost with
//                   rotations; refit; bulk build by binned SAH. The general
//                   choice, and the only one that does not care how big or
//                   how clumped the items are.
//   LooseOctree[T]  octree.cone. Ulrich's loose octree over a fixed cube:
//                   one cell per item, chosen by its size and centre, so
//                   insert, remove and move are O(depth) and need no search.
//                   Good for items of very different sizes in a known world.
//   HashGrid[T]     grid.cone. A uniform hash grid of cubes of one size: the
//                   fastest for very many small, similar things spread evenly
//                   (cheapest build, ray walk and pair finding); poor for
//                   clumps and for things much bigger than a cube.
//
// WHAT AN INDEX DOES (the same names on all three). Boxes are closed:
// touching counts as overlapping.
//
//   insert(box, handle) Proxy          a Proxy is slot plus generation: it
//   remove(proxy) bool                 reaches nothing once its item is gone
//   move(proxy, box, displacement)     whether the structure changed
//   contains(proxy), handle(proxy), box(proxy), len()
//
//   overlapAabb(box, visit)            visit(handle, proxy) -> bool
//   overlapSphere(sphere, visit)       (true goes on, false stops)
//   frustum(&frustum, visit)           not wholly outside (geomath's test)
//   rayCast(ray, maxT, visit)          visit(handle, proxy, t) -> f32: see below
//   rayNearest(ray, maxT) Option[Hit]  the first box entered
//   rayAll(ray, maxT, visit)           visit(handle, proxy, t) -> bool
//   nearest(point, k, &mut out)        k nearest by box distance, nearest first
//
//   allPairs(&mut out)                 every overlapping pair
//   updatePairs(&mut events)           pairs begun and ended since last call
//
// A visitor is a closure held in a var variable and passed as '&mut visitor'.
//
// RAYS. t is a multiple of the ray's direction (no need to normalise); a ray
// starting inside a box enters at 0. A box is the index's only geometry: for
// exact shapes, rayCast's visitor tests the real shape and answers the true
// distance. It answers the reach from then on: the t of the hit just found
// (look only for nearer ones), or FAR to go on through everything, or a
// negative number to stop. rayNearest is rayCast answering t at every hit.
//
// PAIRS. The pair set is kept per item as a diff: after items are inserted
// or move, updatePairs asks only about those, and appends to 'events' the
// pairs that began and ended (a removed item ends its pairs). The first call
// reports every pair as new. On the BVH pairs are of the fat boxes (so a jitter
// inside the margin is no event: Box2D's broad phase); on the octree and grid
// of the boxes themselves; 'pairBox' says which. A Pair holds both handles
// and both proxies, in slot order.
//
// NOTHING IN A QUERY MAY CHANGE THE INDEX: a visitor that inserts, removes or
// moves is a program error.
//
// Written wholly in Cone, over geomath and collections. Files: common.cone
// (proxies, results, ray-box test, plane-masked frustum culling, heap),
// items.cone (item slots, pair bookkeeping), bvhtree.cone (the tree without
// handles), then the three indexes. All one module; only this root imports.

mod spatialindex;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
